/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  /* Control, outside both blocks. */
  create_object WILD_BOAR
  {
    number_of_objects 25
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

  /* Block 1. A range pinned at a hundred against a literal zero. */
  start_random
    percent_chance rnd(100,100)
      create_object OLIVE_TREE
      {
        number_of_objects 40
        set_gaia_object_only
      }
    percent_chance 0
      create_object GOLD
      {
        number_of_objects 40
        set_gaia_object_only
      }
  end_random

  /* Block 2. The mirror. A range pinned at zero against a literal hundred. */
  start_random
    percent_chance rnd(0,0)
      create_object FORAGE
      {
        number_of_objects 40
        set_gaia_object_only
      }
    percent_chance 100
      create_object STONE
      {
        number_of_objects 40
        set_gaia_object_only
      }
  end_random

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_55 - does a random chance branch accept a random-range expression as its
percentage?
Feeds: parser-design Sec.13 item 4, language.json's argument types

QUESTION
The random-range form is documented as usable in numeric slots generally, and
the guide does not enumerate whether the percentage on a random branch is one of
them. The parser accepts it because the slot is typed numeric; nothing has ever
checked that the engine does.

If the engine does NOT evaluate it there, the likely failures are that the
branch never fires, that it always fires, or that the whole random block is
malformed and the script dies - and the parser is currently silent about all
three.

METHOD
Two independent random blocks, each with two branches, each deciding between two
objects. In both blocks the outcome is forced, so nothing here is a coin toss.

  Block 1: a range whose ends are both a hundred, against a literal zero.
           Evaluated -> always the first branch -> 40 trees, no gold.
           Not evaluated as a number -> whatever the engine does with a
           malformed percentage, which is the finding.

  Block 2: a range whose ends are both zero, against a literal hundred.
           Evaluated -> always the SECOND branch -> 40 stone, no bushes.

The two blocks are mirror images on purpose. A single block cannot distinguish
"the range evaluated correctly" from "an unparsed percentage defaults to always
firing", because both give the first branch. Reading the two together does: an
unparsed percentage that always fires gives trees AND bushes, while a correct
evaluation gives trees and stone.

  40 trees + 40 stone      -> the range is evaluated. Confirm the slot type and
                              close the item.
  40 trees + 40 bushes     -> an unparsed percentage always fires. The range is
                              not evaluated and the first branch wins by default.
  40 gold + 40 bushes      -> an unparsed percentage never fires.
  a blank or broken map    -> the construct is fatal, which is the strongest
                              possible reason for the parser to warn on it.

The 25 boars are the control. They sit outside both blocks; if they are missing
the map failed for an unrelated reason.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. THREE RUNS. The outcomes above are
forced rather than probabilistic, so three runs are not for statistics - they
are to catch the case where the engine really is rolling a die and the first
generation happened to look decisive.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>"
  The default histogram is the whole reading.
*/
